items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:32:---
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:33:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:34:## Statement
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:35:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:36:Let $(a_k)$ and $(b_k)$ be sequences of reals, and let $A_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k$ be
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:37:the partial sums of $\sum a_k$ ([[def-series]]). Suppose that
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:38:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:39:1. the range $\{\, A_n : n \in \mathbb{N} \,\}$ is bounded ([[def-bounded-set]]),
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:40:   that is there is a real $M \ge 0$ with $|A_n| \le M$ for every $n$; and
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:41:2. $(b_k)$ is nonincreasing ([[def-monotone-sequence]]) and converges to $0$
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:42:   ([[def-real-limit]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:43:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:44:Then $\sum a_k b_k$ converges.
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:45:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:46:Under hypothesis 2 the terms $b_k$ are automatically nonnegative, and the proof
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:47:says so before using it: a nonincreasing sequence is bounded below by each of its
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:48:own later terms, and passing to the limit gives $b_k \ge 0$
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:49:([[lem-limit-preserves-order]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:50:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:51:**Nothing is assumed about $\sum a_k$ itself.** Its partial sums need only stay
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:52:bounded; they need not converge. That is what makes this test the source of the
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:53:alternating series test ([[thm-alternating-series-test]]) and of examples whose
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:54:sign pattern is not alternating at all.
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:55:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:56:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:57:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:58:**Given:** Sequences $(a_k)$ and $(b_k)$ of reals with $A_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k$ bounded in absolute value, and $(b_k)$ nonincreasing with $b_k \to 0$.
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:59:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:60:[L1] Abel summation by parts: for every $n \ge 1$, $\sum_{k<n} a_k b_k = A_n b_{n-1} - \sum_{k<n-1} A_{k+1}(b_{k+1} - b_k)$ ([[lem-abel-summation-by-parts]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:61:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:62:[L2] Nonincreasing means $b_j \ge b_k$ whenever $j \le k$ ([[def-monotone-sequence]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:63:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:64:[L3] Limits preserve non-strict inequalities holding eventually ([[lem-limit-preserves-order]], [[def-real-limit]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:65:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:66:[L4] Telescoping: with $d_k := b_k - b_{k+1}$, the partial sums of $\sum d_k$ are $b_0 - b_n$, and $\sum d_k$ converges if and only if $(b_k)$ converges, with sum $b_0 - \lim_k b_k$ ([[lem-telescoping-series]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:67:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:68:[L5] Direct comparison: if $0 \le x_k \le y_k$ from some index on and $\sum y_k$ converges, then $\sum x_k$ converges ([[thm-direct-comparison-test]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:69:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:70:[L6] If $\sum |x_k|$ converges then $\sum x_k$ converges ([[lem-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:71:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:72:[L7] Linearity: if $\sum x_k$ converges then so does $\sum c\,x_k$ for every real $c$ ([[lem-series-linearity]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:73:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:74:[L8] A null sequence times a bounded sequence is null ([[lem-null-times-bounded]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:75:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:76:[L9] Algebra of limits for differences of convergent sequences ([[thm-algebra-of-limits]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:77:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:78:[L10] A sequence converges to $x$ if and only if some tail of it converges to $x$ ([[lem-limit-of-tail]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:79:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:80:[L11] Absolute value: $|xy| = |x||y|$, $|x| \ge 0$, and $|-x| = |x|$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:81:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:82:[L12] A bounded set of reals admits a bound in absolute value ([[def-bounded-set]]).
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:83:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:84:## Proof
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:85:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:86:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:87:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:88:1.1 Fix a real $M \ge 0$ with $|A_n| \le M$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$. [given, L12, choose]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:89:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:90:1.2 For each fixed $k$ the inequality $b_m \le b_k$ holds for all $m \ge k$, and $(b_m)_m$ converges to $0$ while the constant sequence with value $b_k$ converges to $b_k$; hence $0 \le b_k$. [given, L2, L3]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:91:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:92:1.3 Put $d_k := b_k - b_{k+1}$ and $c_k := A_{k+1}(b_{k+1} - b_k)$ for $k \in \mathbb{N}$, and let $s_n := \sum_{k<n} a_k b_k$, $t_n := \sum_{k<n} c_k$ and $u_n := A_{n+1} b_n$. [given]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:93:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:94:1.4 Each $d_k \ge 0$, since $(b_k)$ is nonincreasing; and $\sum d_k$ converges, with sum $b_0 - 0 = b_0$, because $(b_k)$ converges to $0$. [given, L2, L4]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:95:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:96:2.1 For every $k$, $|c_k| = |A_{k+1}|\,|b_{k+1} - b_k| = |A_{k+1}|\, d_k \le M d_k$, using $b_{k+1} - b_k = -d_k$ and $d_k \ge 0$. [step 1.1, step 1.3, step 1.4, L11]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:97:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:98:2.2 The sequence $(A_{n+1})_{n}$ is bounded by $M$ and $(b_n)$ converges to $0$, so $u_n = A_{n+1} b_n$ converges to $0$. [step 1.1, step 1.3, given, L8]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:99:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:100:2.3 The series $\sum M d_k$ converges, by step 1.4 and linearity. [step 1.4, L7]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:101:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:102:2.4 For every $n \in \mathbb{N}$, applying [L1] at the index $n+1 \ge 1$ gives $s_{n+1} = A_{n+1} b_n - \sum_{k<n} A_{k+1}(b_{k+1}-b_k) = u_n - t_n$. [step 1.3, L1]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:103:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:104:3.1 Since $0 \le |c_k| \le M d_k$ for every $k$, the series $\sum |c_k|$ converges by comparison, and therefore $\sum c_k$ converges; write $T$ for its sum, so that $t_n \to T$. [step 2.1, step 2.3, L5, L6]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:105:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:106:4.1 By step 2.2, step 3.1 and the algebra of limits, $s_{n+1} \to 0 - T = -T$ as $n \to \infty$. [step 2.2, step 3.1, step 2.4, L9]
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:107:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:108:5.1 The sequence $(s_{n+1})_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ is the first tail of $(s_n)$, so $(s_n)$ itself converges to $-T$; that is, $\sum a_k b_k$ converges, with sum $-T$. [step 4.1, L10] ∎
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:109:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:110:## Remarks
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:111:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:112:- **Where each hypothesis is used, and none is decorative.** Boundedness of $(A_n)$ is used twice: once to bound $|c_k|$ in step 2.1, and once to kill the boundary term in step 2.2. Monotonicity of $(b_k)$ is what makes $|b_{k+1} - b_k|$ equal to $b_k - b_{k+1}$, so that the bound in step 2.1 telescopes; without it the differences need not sum to anything. And $b_k \to 0$ is used both in the telescoping sum of step 1.4 and in the boundary term of step 2.2.
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:113:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:114:- **Why *nonincreasing* and not *monotone*, although either would do.** Hypothesis 2 could equally be stated with "monotone", and the theorem would still be true: a nondecreasing $(b_k)$ converging to $0$ is nonpositive, so $(-b_k)$ is nonincreasing and converges to $0$, and applying the theorem to it gives convergence of $\sum a_k(-b_k)$ and hence of $\sum a_k b_k$ ([[lem-series-linearity]]). What "monotone" may not be weakened to is "monotone and bounded": a monotone $(b_k)$ with a nonzero limit is not covered, and for such a factor the conclusion fails in general. The nonincreasing form is chosen here because it is the form the proof uses, and because it makes $b_k \ge 0$ immediate. [[thm-abel-test]] is the result that handles monotone bounded factors, and it has a different hypothesis on $\sum a_k$.
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:115:
items/thm-dirichlet-test.md:116:- **The sum is not computed.** The proof produces the limit as $-T$, where $T$ is the sum of a series that the argument only proves convergent. This is a convergence test and nothing more.
