items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:35:---
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:36:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:37:## Definition
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:38:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:39:Let $(a_k)$ be a sequence of reals, with series $\sum a_k$ and partial sums
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:40:$s_n = \sum_{k<n} a_k$ as in [[def-series]], and let $|x|$ be the absolute value
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:41:([[def-abs-value]]).
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:42:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:43:**Absolute convergence.** The series $\sum a_k$ **converges absolutely** when the
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:44:series $\sum |a_k|$ converges ([[def-series]]). Since $|a_k| \ge 0$ for every $k$
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:45:([[lem-of-abs-value]]), this is a statement about a series of nonnegative terms.
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:46:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:47:**Conditional convergence.** The series $\sum a_k$ **converges conditionally**
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:48:when it converges ([[def-series]], [[def-real-limit]]) and does *not* converge
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:49:absolutely.
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:50:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:51:So a convergent series is exactly one of the two: absolutely convergent or
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:52:conditionally convergent, according as $\sum |a_k|$ converges or not.
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:53:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:54:**One implication is already proved, and is not reproved anywhere on this page.**
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:55:[[lem-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence]] states that if $\sum |a_k|$
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:56:converges then $\sum a_k$ converges. That lemma was coined and proved on the
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:57:previous page of this track, where the root and ratio tests need it; this page
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:58:names it and builds on it. In particular an absolutely convergent series is a
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:59:convergent series, so the two words above really do partition the convergent
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:60:series, and "conditionally convergent" is not vacuous by accident: the alternating
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:61:harmonic series is a witness, and the witness is exhibited in
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:62:[[fs-convergent-implies-absolutely-convergent]].
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:63:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:64:**General starting index.** Let $m \in \mathbb{N}$ and let $(a_k)_{k \ge m}$ be a
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:65:family from $m$ ([[def-series]]). The series $\sum_{k \ge m} a_k$ **converges
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:66:absolutely** when $\sum_{k \ge m} |a_k|$ converges, and **converges
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:67:conditionally** when it converges and does not converge absolutely. By
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:68:[[def-series]] both statements are the corresponding statements for the shifted
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:69:sequence $j \mapsto a_{j+m}$, so nothing new is being defined and every result
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:70:below transfers to a general starting index in the same way, exactly as
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:71:[[lem-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence]] already records for the one
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:72:implication it proves.
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:73:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:74:## Remarks
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:75:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:76:- **Absolute convergence is a condition on the terms, not on the sum.** It says
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:77:  the series of absolute values converges, and it says nothing about the value of
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:78:  $\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k$. The two sums are in general different, and no
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:79:  statement here identifies them.
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:80:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:81:- **Why the distinction earns a page.** Every result on this page separates the
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:82:  two classes. An absolutely convergent series may be reordered at will
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:83:  ([[thm-dirichlet-rearrangement]]) and multiplied by another
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:84:  ([[thm-mertens]]); a conditionally convergent one may be reordered to any sum
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:85:  whatever ([[thm-riemann-series-theorem]]). The difference is not one of degree.
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:86:
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:87:- **A series of nonnegative terms converges absolutely if it converges at all**,
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:88:  since then $|a_k| = a_k$. So the distinction is invisible for the comparison,
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:89:  condensation, Raabe, Gauss and Kummer tests of the previous page, all of which
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:90:  assume terms of one sign. It is not invisible on that page as a whole: the root
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:91:  and ratio tests are stated for terms of arbitrary sign and reach convergence of
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:92:  $\sum a_k$ precisely through
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:93:  [[lem-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence]], which is where the word
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:94:  *absolutely convergent* is first used. What that page does not develop, and this
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:95:  one does, is everything that separates the two classes rather than the one
items/def-absolute-and-conditional-convergence.md:96:  implication those two tests need.

